/**
  @mainpage Plugin API Documentation

  MariaDB Server Plugin API is a C-based extensibility framework
  (largely compatible with MySQL’s but extended) that allows dynamic or
  static loading of modules to add or modify server behavior without
  recompiling the core server.

   - @subpage concepts
   - @subpage implementation
   - @subpage make_a_plugin
   - @ref plugin_types
   - @ref plugin_api_services_for_plugins
   - [API reference](api.md#)
*/

/**
  @page concepts Plugin API Concepts

  @section concept_big_picture The Big Picture: The Server, Plugins and Interfaces

  The server does, at certain stages of its execution, call plugin APIs when it
  needs certain operation performed: e.g. authenticate a user, log a message,
  read and write from/to a database table etc.

  These API are like written functionality contracts.
  They allow separating the server functionality from the actual API
  implementations themselves. And, since the API implementations are a dynamic set,
  adding new implementations extends the server functionality
  (e.g. adds new ways a user can be authenticated or message be logged)
  without having to re-compile and even re-start the server itself.

  The MariaDB server plugin infrastructure uses the following logical terms:

  @section concept_plugin_api Plugin API

  A well defined, versioned and stable interface specification.
  It's also abstract, as it's not tied to any one specific implementation.

  @section concept_plugin Plugin

  A named and versioned implementation of a specific @ref concept_plugin_api.

  @sa @ref plugin_types for a list of the available plugin types and what they do

  @section concept_plugin_library Plugin Library

  (habitually abbreviated as a plugin)
  An executable binary that contains one or more @ref concept_plugin that can
  be accessed by the server when it needs to call plugin API implementations.

  @section concept_plugin_service Plugin Services

  Sometimes plugin code needs to call back into the server.

  The plugin is running into the same process and the server symbols are
  not hidden. So it can just reference a server symbol: e.g. call a server
  function or read a server variable. These will get resolved by the OS's
  dynamic linker (for dynamic plugins) or static linker (for static plugins).

  But this is considered **bad** since there's no guarantee that the plugin's
  snapshot of what the server symbol is captured during plugin compilation
  matches the actual server symbol definition inside the running server.

  This is why the plugin infrastructure provides a more controlled way for a
  plugin to call server code.

  It's called a plugin service.

  Plugin services are a finite set determined at server's and plugin's
  compile time.

  They guarantee that the service is present, matches the definition and
  is callable after plugin load.
  They also provide platform independence as they operate the same on all
  OSes and architectures.

  @section concept_plugin_system_variables Plugin System Variables

  Plugins can provide their own configuration variables. These become
  regular server system variables when the plugin is loaded: their values
  can be stored in config files or passed on the server's command line or
  via the SET command.
  When the plugin is unloaded the variables go away with it.

  The variable names known to the server are prefixed with the plugin name
  so that they do not pollute the namespace. E.g. the variable `foo` becomes
  known to the server as `bar_plugin_foo` when exported by the plugin `bar_plugin`.

  Each variable has a declared data type.

  Plugin authors must decide if they want to keep a single global value
  or a per-session value + global value for each variable.

  Plugin authors must also decide if they will allow setting the value at
  runtime or not. And make sure the plugin code is built to handle value changes
  mid-flight if they allow it.

  @section concept_plugin_status_variables Plugin Status Variables

  Plugins can also provide their own status variables. Think of status
  variables as read-only @ref concept_plugin_system_variables that the plugin
  authors can use to expose volatile data of the plugin state, like e.g. counters,
  on/off indicators, gauges, progress bars, state names etc.

  Status variables are also lighter weight compared to @ref concept_plugin_system_variables :
  knowing that they're read-only allows better optimization and less locking when handling them.
  So, if plugin authors can use status variables, it's advisable that they should.

  Similarly to system variables, the status variables become known to the server
  when the plugin is loaded and go away when it's unloaded.

  @warning Status variables are not automatically prefixed with the plugin name! You will need
  to prefix your status variables with the plugin name.
  See @ref impl_plugin_status_variables_array for a convenient way to do this.

  The status variables also have a declared data type.

  @sa @ref implementation on how the above concepts are implemented.

*/

/**
  @page implementation Plugin API Implementation

  This is how the individual abstract concepts are implemented and
  interact with each other.

  @section impl_server The Server Plugin API implementation

  The server is the bedrock of the plugin infrastructure and contains the
  base implementation to enable plugin usage.
  Plugins can theoretically be loaded into other binaries, but chances are
  that they won't operate all that well (if at all) in this case since they
  heavily depend on server's infrastructure.

  Here's what the server implements to support plugins:

  @subsection impl_server_global_plugin_list The Global Plugin List

  The server maintains a global in-memory list of all available plugins (plugin_array), mirrored into a set of hash tables (plugin_hash) and guarded by LOCK_plugin.

  The plugin libraries containing the available plugins can be:
   - statically linked into the server binary
   - dynamically loaded at startup via the --plugin-load... command line arguments
   - dynamically loaded just prior to the server's commencement of normal operations from the mysql.plugins table.
   - dynamically loaded at runtime via the INSTALL PLUGIN SQL command.
   - unloaded at runtime via the UNINSTALL PLUGIN SQL command
   - unloaded at server shutdown

  The server implements all of the above possibilities in its code.

  It also implements and provides a list of interfaces the plugins can use to
  call back and interact with the server.
  These are called @ref impl_server_plugin_services.

  The list and some basic attributes for each plugin in it are visible via
  the INFORMATION_SCHEMA.plugins table.

  @subsection impl_server_plugin_api_call How Does The Server Call a Plugin API.

  At certain opportune stages of server's normal operation the server might decide
  to "reach out" to a plugin by calling a plugin API. Since the plugins are named it
  can call any individual plugin by name. E.g. when executing `CREATE TABLE engine=foo`,
  the server will:

  - look "foo" into the cache of plugin "references" (plugin_ref) for that plugin type, if available.
  - If no cached reference is found:
      - Take LOCK_plugin
      - Look "foo" up into the global plugin_array for implementations of the specific plugin API (storage engine in this case)
      - If found, create a reference (plugin_ref) to the plugin.
  - call API "method" (function pointers) through the plugin_ref, if found.
  - when done, either store the plugin_ref into a cache for reuse or release it.

  @section impl_server_plugin_services Plugin Services Implementation

  Every dynamic plugin exposes one symbol for every service it knows about. These
  are implemented by the `libservices` static library that is linked into every plugin.

  Initially, these global pointers are not valid pointers: when the plugin is compiled,
  they are set to the service version (e.g. VERSION_base64) that the plugin expects.
  The service header also contains a definition of a more concrete data structure
  (e.g. @ref base64_service_st) that this global pointer should be pointing to.

  The server maintains a global named list of plugin service API implementations.
  Every time a @ref concept_plugin_library is initialized, the server gets its global
  service API implementation list, looks up all the symbols exported by the
  @ref concept_plugin_library, and, for each symbol:

  - checks the versions by comparing the symbol's value to the server version.
  - sets @ref concept_plugin_library 's symbol value to the actual server
    service pointer.

  Each service header also contains "syntax sugar" macros, e.g.

  ```
  #define my_base64_encode(A,B,C) base64_service->base64_encode_ptr(A,B,C)
  ```

  that allow successful compilation of

  ```
    my_base64_encode(x,y,z)
  ```
  as a function pointer call through the @ref concept_plugin_library 's service pointer.

  @sa @ref plugin_api_services for a list of the services provided by the server.

  @section impl_plugin_library The Plugin library Implementation

  A @ref concept_plugin_library is executable code, either a part of
  the server itself or bundled as a shared library.

  Each plugin library implements the plugin library API. It exposes that implementation
  via a set of dynamic library symbols.

  @subsection impl_plugin_library_static Statically Linked Plugin Libraries.

  Plugins linked statically to the server are collected into a global
  server "plugin library" and "installed" (and initialized) at server startup.

  @subsection impl_plugin_library_dynamic Dynamic Plugin Libraries

  A @ref concept_plugin_library is usually compiled as OS dynamic library:
   DLLs on MS Windows, .so on Unixes.
  What differentiates a @ref concept_plugin_library from a regular OS
  shared library is that it exposes a specific public symbol
  _maria_plugin_declarations_ that the server recognizes.
  In fact it also exposes _maria_plugin_interface_version_ and
  _maria_sizeof_struct_st_plugin_, but these are auxiliary.

  See @ref maria_declare_plugin for details on how this is implemented.

  @subsection impl_plugin_library_lifecycle The Lifecycle Of a Plugin Library

  A plugin library always provides an array of @ref st_maria_plugin structures,
  each describing a @ref concept_plugin this @ref concept_plugin_library provides.
  This contains everything that goes between @ref maria_declare_plugin and
  @ref maria_declare_plugin_end in plugin declaration.

  When the @ref concept_plugin_library is loaded into the server:

   - the library is version and maturity checked. @sa @subpage plugin_verification
   - for each @ref concept_plugin contained
     - plugin API version check is performed
     - the plugin system variables are added the variable global lists
     - the plugin is initialized
     - the status variables are added to the variable global lists
     - the plugin is added into the global plugin_array.

  At unload time the effect of plugin library load above is reversed.

  @section impl_plugin Plugin implementation

  A MariaDB plugin is a logical, versioned implementation of exactly two interfaces:
   - The base, house-keeping @ref st_maria_plugin plugin management interface
   - A specific plugin interface, one of @ref plugin_types. This is referenced by the
     @ref st_maria_plugin::info pointer of the @ref st_maria_plugin structure.

  @section impl_plugin_system_variables Plugin System Variables Implementation

  Broadly speaking the @ref concept_plugin_system_variables are a property of the @ref st_maria_plugin
  interface that every plugin exposes. They are assigned to the
  @ref st_maria_plugin::system_vars member. When the plugin is loaded the data
  in that NULL-terminated C++ array are used to register the system variables into
  the server. And also to unregister them at plugin unload time.

  The plugin macros @ref plugin_sysvar_macros, @ref plugin_thdvar_macros and @ref MYSQL_SYSVAR
  facilitate defining an array of `st_mysql_sys_var` system variable definitions
  that is then passed as a value initializer to @ref st_maria_plugin::system_vars member
  when defining the plugin.

  But before we can examine more practical examples, there are some important decisions
  to make about each system variable: it's type and settability cases.

  @subsection impl_plugin_sysvars_type Decide on Data Type and Settability.

  @subsubsection impl_plugin_sysvars_type_data_type Pick a Data Type

  The data types available are listed in the @ref plugin_sysvar_types group.
  Pick the one that matches your configuration option best.

  @subsubsection impl_plugin_sysvars_type_settability Pick Settability

  You need to decide if the system variable should just be a global variable or
  if it also should have a per-session value.

  The global variable represents a single variable instance, containing a single value,
  shared across all worker threads inside the server. Think global scope C/C++ variables.

  The per-session variable, in addition to what the global variable does,
  will also allow:
   - lock-free access to the session value.
   - Every session having a different value for this variable, that is defaulted
  to the current value of the global variable at session creation time.

  See the corresponding server reference manual chapter on system variables for more
  details on this.

  @subsection impl_plugin_sysvars_callbacks Check and Update Callbacks

  @subsubsection impl_plugin_sysvars_callbacks_check The Check Callback

  This callback (if non-`NULL`) is called by the server **before** the variable
  value is set. See @ref mysql_var_check_func for the parameters of the callback.
  This is the plugin author's chance to check the new value and return an error if it
  cannot or should not be set.

  This callback is expected to:
   - inspect the value passed to it
   - apply validity constraints (e.g. limits), if any to the value.
   - convert it to the correct native C type (see @ref plugin_sysvar_types) for the
     destination value.
   - store it (in native C format) into the temp storage the save pointer points to

  If no callback is supplied the server adds its own for each system variable type
  supported.

  A very simple check function for the @ref PLUGIN_VAR_DOUBLE type is shown below:

  ```
  static int check_func_double(THD *thd, struct st_mysql_sys_var *var,
                               void *save, st_mysql_value *value)
  {
    double v;
    const double v_max = 100.0, v_min=-100.0;

    if (value->val_real(value, &v))
      return 1;
    if (v < v_min || v > v_max)
      return 1;
    *(double *) save= v;

    return 0;
  }
  ```

  @subsubsection impl_plugin_sysvars_callbacks_update The Update Callback

  This callback (if non-`NULL`) is called by the server to set the new value into
  the variable. See @ref mysql_var_update_func for the parameters to the callback.
  
  The plugin author should implement that if they want to have more control over how
  the variable is set. E.g. if they want to store the value into an atomic variable
  they will need the update callback. 

  Another reason to implement is to invoke any side effects on setting the variable.
  E.g. if a new value for a temp cache size is set, the code would need to actually
  resize the global cache list to the new size in addition to storing the new size value.

  This callback is expected to:
    - fetch the value to be set stored by @ref impl_plugin_sysvars_callbacks_check
    - store it into the variable pointed to by the `var_ptr` pointer argument

  If no callback is supplied the server adds its own for each system variable type
  supported.

  A very simple update function for the @ref PLUGIN_VAR_DOUBLE type is shown below:

  ```
  static void update_func_double(THD *thd, struct st_mysql_sys_var *var,
                                 void *tgt, const void *save)
  {
    *(double *) tgt= *(double *) save;
  }
  ```

  @subsection impl_plugin_sysvars_global Define a Global Variable

  To define a global only variable, do as follows:

  - Define the C++ global variable. Use the variable type for your selected type mentioned
    in @ref plugin_sysvar_types, e.g.
    ```
      char *var1_var;
      char default_var1_var[]= "var1 default";
      ...
    ```
  - Use one of the @ref plugin_sysvar_macros to declare the variable, e.g.
    ```
      static MYSQL_SYSVAR_STR(var1, var1_var, PLUGIN_VAR_RQCMDARG,
       "Variable 1", NULL, NULL, default_var1_var);
    ```
  - Add the variable to a global per-plugin NULL terminated C++ array using @ref MYSQL_SYSVAR, e.g. 
    ```
       static struct st_mysql_sys_var* vars[] = {
	  ...
	  MYSQL_SYSVAR(var1),
	  ...
	  NULL
       }
    ```
  -  Pass the array to @ref maria_declare_plugin, e.g.
    ```
      maria_declare_plugin(innobase)
      {
        MYSQL_STORAGE_ENGINE_PLUGIN,
        &innobase_storage_engine,
        innobase_hton_name,
        plugin_author,
        "Supports transactions, row-level locking, foreign keys and encryption for tables",
        PLUGIN_LICENSE_GPL,
        innodb_init, // Plugin Init
        NULL, // Plugin Deinit
        MYSQL_VERSION_MAJOR << 8 | MYSQL_VERSION_MINOR,
        innodb_status_variables_export, // status variables
        vars,   // system variables
        PACKAGE_VERSION,
        MariaDB_PLUGIN_MATURITY_STABLE // maturity
      }
    ```

  @subsection impl_plugin_sysvars_session Define a Session Variable

  Unlike the global ones, session variables do not need a C++ placeholder variable.
  The global value is stored within a global server data structure. And the values
  for each session are stored into a per-session data structure, owned by the 
  @ref MYSQL_THD structure.

  This also means that you will need to use the @ref THDVAR macro to access both
  the global (pass `NULL` to the first @ref MYSQL_THD argument) and the per-session values.

  Example:

  ```
     static MYSQL_THDVAR_INT(tvar, ...);
     ...
     // Get and set the global value
     int val = THDVAR(NULL, tvar);
     THDVAR(NULL, tvar)= 12;
     ...
     // Get and set the per-thread value
     MYSQL_THD thd = ...
     int val = THDVAR(thd, tvar);
     THDVAR(thd, tvar)= 42;
     ...
  ```

  To define a global+session variable do as follows:

  - Define the variable using one of the @ref plugin_thdvar_macros, e.g.:
    ```
      static MYSQL_THDVAR_INT(tvar,
        PLUGIN_VAR_RQCMDARG, // flags
        "Default depth used by Json, XML and Mongo discovery", // comment
        NULL, // check
        NULL, // update
        5, // default
        -1, // min
        16, // max
        1); // block.
    ```
  - Add the variable to a global per-plugin NULL terminated C++ array using @ref MYSQL_SYSVAR, e.g. 
    ```
       static struct st_mysql_sys_var* vars[] = {
          ...
          MYSQL_SYSVAR(tvar),
          ...
          NULL
       }
    ```
  -  Pass the array to @ref maria_declare_plugin, e.g.
    ```
      maria_declare_plugin(innobase)
      {
        MYSQL_STORAGE_ENGINE_PLUGIN,
        &innobase_storage_engine,
        innobase_hton_name,
        plugin_author,
        "Supports transactions, row-level locking, foreign keys and encryption for tables",
        PLUGIN_LICENSE_GPL,
        innodb_init, // Plugin Init
        NULL, // Plugin Deinit
        MYSQL_VERSION_MAJOR << 8 | MYSQL_VERSION_MINOR,
        innodb_status_variables_export, // status variables
        vars,   // system variables
        PACKAGE_VERSION,
        MariaDB_PLUGIN_MATURITY_STABLE // maturity
      }
    ```

    @note You can mix global and session variable into a single array.

  @section impl_plugin_status_variables Plugin Status Variables Implementation

  @ref concept_plugin_status_variables are defined similarly to
  @ref concept_plugin_system_variables by creating a per-plugin `NULL` terminated
  array of @ref st_mysql_show_var structures and passing it down
  to @ref maria_declare_plugin.

  Unlike the system variables, the status variables are read-only, i.e. only
  the plugin code can manipulate the status variable's value.

  Each status variable has a type: one of the @ref enum_mysql_show_type types.
  In practice try to restrict your variables to use either @ref SHOW_LONG or
  @ref SHOW_LONGLONG. These are typically counters and these two types get
  reset by the server when you reset status. Plus, they are typically accessed
  atomically by modern CPUs so you can skip doing locking. 

  The scope for the status variables is handled by the server as follows:
  All of the status variables data types except @ref SHOW_FUNC, SHOW_FUNC_ENTRY
  and @ref SHOW_ARRAY are treated as global+session status values. 
  This means that each session gets a private value slot that is aggregated into
  the global value as the session ends.
  The rest of them are implemented as global scope: a single global status
  variable that all of the threads are reading from and writing to.

  Status variables can be scalar or functional.

  @subsection impl_plugin_status_variables_scalar Scalar Status Variables

  For "scalar" status variables the data is kept into a C/C++ variable and
  a pointer to that is passed to the infrastructure. When the server needs to
  read the value it just de-references the pointer and reads the value.

  To implement a scalar status variable do as follows:

  - Define the global counter variable
    ```
      unsigned long stat_var_buffer;
    ```
  - Add it to a global per-plugin NULL-element terminated array:
    ```
      static struct st_mysql_show_var status_variables[]= {
        ...
        {"stat_var", (char *) &stat_var_buffer, SHOW_LONGLONG},
        ...
        {NullS, NullS, SHOW_LONG}};
    ```
  - Pass the global array to @ref maria_declare_plugin :
     ```
      maria_declare_plugin(innobase)
      {
        MYSQL_STORAGE_ENGINE_PLUGIN,
        &innobase_storage_engine,
        innobase_hton_name,
        plugin_author,
        "Supports transactions, row-level locking, foreign keys and encryption for tables",
        PLUGIN_LICENSE_GPL,
        innodb_init, // Plugin Init
        NULL, // Plugin Deinit
        MYSQL_VERSION_MAJOR << 8 | MYSQL_VERSION_MINOR,
        status variables, // status variables
        vars,   // system variables
        PACKAGE_VERSION,
        MariaDB_PLUGIN_MATURITY_STABLE // maturity
      }
    ```
  - Manipulate the global counter variable
    ```
       stat_var_buffer++;
    ```

  @subsection impl_plugin_status_variables_function Functional Status Variables

  For functional status variables instead of dereferencing a pointer, the server
  takes a function pointer of the type @ref mysql_show_var_func and is calling
  the function when it needs the value.

  The function can also change the type for the variable.

  A typical @ref SHOW_SIMPLE_FUNC variable looks like this:
  ```
    static int show_func_example(MYSQL_THD thd, struct st_mysql_show_var *var,
                             void *buf, system_status_var *, enum_var_type)
    {
      var->type= SHOW_CHAR;
      var->value= buf; // it's of SHOW_VAR_FUNC_BUFF_SIZE bytes
      my_snprintf((char*) buf, SHOW_VAR_FUNC_BUFF_SIZE,
              ...);
      return 0;
    }
    ...
    static struct st_mysql_show_var func_status[]=
    {
      {"func_example",  (char *)show_func_example, SHOW_SIMPLE_FUNC},
      {0,0,SHOW_UNDEF}
    };
  ```

  There's also @ref SHOW_FUNC, which you can use through the SHOW_FUNC_ENTRY
  macro as follows:
  ```
    static int show_value(THD *thd, SHOW_VAR *var, void *buff,
                           system_status_var *, enum_var_type scope)
    {
      var->type= SHOW_ULONGLONG;
      var->value= buff;
      *(ulonglong*) buff= 0;
      return 0;
    }
    ...
    static struct st_mysql_show_var func_status[]=
    {
      SHOW_FUNC_ENTRY("show_value", &show_value),
      {0,0,SHOW_UNDEF}
    }
  ```

  @subsection impl_plugin_status_variables_array Array Status Variables

  Sometimes you want a group of status variables to have a custom prefix. 

  One approach is to copy that prefix to all the variables in the list, e.g.
  ```
    struct st_mysql_show_var status_variables[] = {
      {"prefix_var1", (char *) &var1, SHOW_LONGLONG},
      {"prefix_var2", (char *) &var2, SHOW_LONGLONG},
      {NullS, NullS, SHOW_LONG}
    };
  ```

  This can be tedious, so MariaDB supports a trick to specify the prefix
  just once:

  ```
    struct st_mysql_show_var plugin_variables[] = {
      {"var1", (char *) &var1, SHOW_LONGLONG},
      {"var2", (char *) &var2, SHOW_LONGLONG},
      {NullS, NullS, SHOW_LONG}
    };

    static int show_plugin_vars(THD *thd, SHOW_VAR *var, void *buff,
                             struct system_status_var *,
                             enum enum_var_type) {
      var->type = SHOW_ARRAY;
      var->value = reinterpret_cast<char *>(&plugin_variables);
      return 0;
    }

    struct st_mysql_show_var status_variables[] = {
      SHOW_FUNC_ENTRY("prefix", &show_plugin_vars),
      {NullS, NullS, SHOW_LONG}
    };
  ```

  This is using the @ref SHOW_FUNC type that prefixes the variable
  names with the prefix supplied.
*/

/**
  @page plugin_verification Plugin Verification

  When a dynamically-loaded @ref concept_plugin_library is loaded into the server,
  the server will verify if it is compatible with it before proceeding any further.
  This is important since the plugin API expects certain data structures to be laid
  out in a certain way so that the plugin can function correctly.

  @section plugin_verification_version Plugin Version Verification

  The server will read plugin's symbol `_maria_plugin_interface_version_`
  (as defined by @ref maria_declare_plugin) and compare that with its own plugin API
  version.
  If plugin's API interface version is with a different major version loading
  is refused.
*/

/**
   @page make_a_plugin How to make a plugin

   This is a tutorial and a walk-through on how to bootstrap, implement, compile,
   test and distribute a MariaDB @ref concept_plugin_library.

   @section map_architecture Architecture and Design Decisions.

   The most important architectural decision is to decide what
   @ref concept_plugin_api to implement.
   To make that determination you need to understand when your
   plugin needs to be called by the server and with what parameters.

   Sometimes just the base @ref st_maria_plugin API is good enough.
   @ref st_maria_plugin::init is called when the plugin is loaded and
   @ref st_maria_plugin::deinit is called when the plugin is unloaded.
   This will suffice if you are after implementing e.g. a network listener:
   you can start a background thread that listens for connections and
   processes connects, disconnects and commands in @ref st_maria_plugin::init.
   And then signal to it to shut down gracefully from @ref st_maria_plugin::deinit.

   In this case you implement the so called @ref daemon_plugin_data. This has no
   API whatsoever. Just a version.

   But, in general, you need to understand the available @ref plugin_types and
   decide on implementing the one that suits your needs best.

   Do you want to provide a better password validation method? Look into
   @ref password_validation_plugin_data.
   Need to expose data in tabular format to server? Do a
   @ref information_schema_plugin_data.

   The good part is that you can actually combine multiple @ref concept_plugin
   into a single @ref concept_plugin_library.

   So, back to the example above, if you need to implement a network listener,
   but also return the current connection table as an INFORMATION_SCHEMA table
   you can define the two @ref concept_plugin implementations into your single
   @ref concept_plugin_library.

   @section map_code Code your plugin library

   It's easiest to just take one of the existing plugins and copy its code.

   But, in case you want to start afresh, here's a very minimal boilerplate to
   copy from
   ```
    #include <mysql/plugin.h>
    /* TODO: if needed: #include <mysql/specific plugin type, e.g. plugin_audit.h> */

    static int plugin_one_init(void *)
    {
      /* TODO: code here */
      return 0; /* success */
    }

    static int plugin_one_deinit(void *)
    {
      /* TODO: code here */
      return 0; /* success */
    }

    static struct st_mysql_daemon plugin_one =
    { MYSQL_DAEMON_INTERFACE_VERSION  };

    maria_declare_plugin(plugin_one)
    {
      MYSQL_DAEMON_PLUGIN,
      &plugin_one,
      "plugin example",
      "You",
      "A boilerplate plugin code",
      PLUGIN_LICENSE_GPL,
      plugin_one_init,            /* Plugin Init */
      plugin_one_deinit,          /* Plugin Deinit */
      0x0100,                     /* 1.0 */
      NULL,                       /* status variables */
      NULL,                       /* system variables */
      "1.0",                      /* string version */
      MariaDB_PLUGIN_MATURITY_EXPERIMENTAL /* maturity */
    }
    maria_declare_plugin_end;
   ```

   @sa @ref maria_declare_plugin, @ref maria_declare_plugin_end,
    @ref MYSQL_DAEMON_PLUGIN, @ref MYSQL_DAEMON_INTERFACE_VERSION,
    @ref PLUGIN_LICENSE_GPL, @ref MariaDB_PLUGIN_MATURITY_EXPERIMENTAL

   If you need to add system variables, you can add the following to the above:

   ```
   static unsigned int v1;

   static int
   v1_check_function(MYSQL_THD thd,
                     struct st_mysql_sys_var *var,
                     void *save, struct st_mysql_value *value) {
     /* TODO: code here */
     return 0; /* OK */
   }
   static void
   v1_update_function(MYSQL_THD thd,
                      struct st_mysql_sys_var *var,
                      void *var_ptr, const void *save) {
     /* TODO: code here */
   }

   static MYSQL_SYSVAR_UINT(
	v1,			/* SQL variable name */
	v1,			/* local C variable reference */
	PLUGIN_VAR_RQCMDARG,    /* flags: An argument is required */
        "gizmo variable",	/* SQL visible description */
	v1_check_function,
	v1_update_function,
        0,                      /* default value */
	0,                      /* minimal value */
	365*100,                /* maximum value */
	1			/* increase step */
  );

  static struct st_mysql_sys_var* sysvars[]= {
    MYSQL_SYSVAR(v1),   /* variable reference */
    NULL                /* terminator. keep */
  };
  ```

  And then pass on the `sysvars` to the @ref maria_declare_plugin 's
  system variable placeholder.

  @sa @ref MYSQL_SYSVAR_UINT, @ref mysql_var_check_func,
  @ref mysql_var_update_func, @ref MYSQL_SYSVAR

  Similarly, if you need status variables, you can add the following:

  ```
  static volatile int var_s1;
  static struct st_mysql_show_var status_vars[]=
  {
    {
      "s1",               /* status variable SQL name */
      (char *) &var_s1,   /* reference to the C variable */
      SHOW_INT            /* type */
    },
    { 0, 0, 0}            /* terminator. keep */
  }
  ```

  And then pass on the `status_vars` to the @ref maria_declare_plugin 's
  status variable placeholder.

  @sa @ref st_mysql_show_var, @ref SHOW_INT
*/
